+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report: httparse 1.10.1
+
+Property-based tests written with [hegel](https://hegel.dev) (`hegeltest` 0.28.2),
+added to the existing unit-test module in `src/lib.rs` as
+`tests::hegel_props` (15 tests).
+
+Note: `hegeltest` was added with `cargo add --dev hegeltest --ignore-rust-version`
+because its MSRV is newer than the crate's `rust-version = "1.59"`. This only
+affects dev-dependencies; whether that is acceptable upstream is a maintainer
+decision.
+
+Context: httparse is a heavily-fuzzed, security-sensitive crate that already
+ships six libFuzzer targets (`fuzz/fuzz_targets/`) covering "no panic on raw
+arbitrary bytes" for `Request::parse`, `Response::parse`, `parse_headers` and
+`parse_chunk_size` (plus the multi-space configs). The properties below were
+chosen to go *beyond* that baseline: HTTP-shaped inputs built by
+generate-valid-then-corrupt, oracle-independent grammar/offset invariants on
+every `Complete` parse, an incremental-vs-whole differential, exact
+round-trips, and boundary-of-the-byte-set rejection checks.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+1. **`request_parse_never_panics_on_arbitrary_bytes`** /
+2. **`response_parse_never_panics_on_arbitrary_bytes`**. Raw drawn bytes (size
+   drawn separately, up to 300+) with a drawn header-slice size 0..=4
+   (including the zero-capacity slice). `parse` must return
+   Ok(Complete)/Ok(Partial)/Err, never panic; any `Complete(n)` is routed
+   through the shared invariant checker (below). Mirrors the fuzz targets but
+   varies the header capacity, which the fuzz targets fix at 16.
+
+3. **`http_shaped_bytes_never_panic_and_invariants_hold`**. A valid drawn
+   request *or* response (leading empty lines, drawn method/path/version or
+   version/code/reason, 0-8 headers, CRLF vs bare-LF line endings) with 0..=4
+   drawn corruptions (byte replace / insert / delete / truncate), parsed as
+   *both* a request and a response. Never panics; on `Complete(n)` the
+   invariant checker asserts, oracle-independently:
+   - `n <= input.len()`;
+   - method/path/version (resp. version/code/reason) are all `Some`;
+   - every returned `&str`/`&[u8]` (method, path, reason, header names and
+     values) is a genuine subslice of the input buffer, checked by pointer
+     range (empty slices exempt — the parser legitimately returns a static
+     `""` for absent/obs-text reasons);
+   - token sets honored: method bytes satisfy `is_method_token`, path bytes
+     `is_uri_token`, header names non-empty and all `is_header_name_token`,
+     header values all `is_header_value_token`, reason bytes HTAB/SP/VCHAR,
+     version <= 1, code <= 999;
+   - no more headers returned than provided slots.
+
+4. **`parse_headers_never_panics_and_offsets_valid`**. Same invariants for the
+   standalone `parse_headers` entry point, fed either a corrupted drawn
+   header block or raw bytes; `Complete((pos, headers))` implies
+   `pos <= len` and in-range, token-valid headers.
+
+5. **`request_roundtrip`**. A well-formed request built from drawn tokens
+   (method from the crate's own `TOKEN_MAP`, path from the URI byte set
+   including non-ASCII UTF-8, version 0/1, headers with names from
+   `TOKEN_MAP` and values from `HEADER_VALUE_MAP`) parses to
+   `Complete(len)` with exactly the drawn method/path/version and headers.
+   Header values are compared modulo the documented leading/trailing
+   SP/HTAB trim.
+
+6. **`response_roundtrip`**. Mirror for responses: version/code round-trip
+   exactly; the reason-phrase round-trips when it is pure HTAB/SP/VCHAR and
+   becomes `""` when absent or containing obs-text bytes (>= 0x80), exactly
+   as the `Response::reason` docs state.
+
+7. **`request_every_prefix_is_partial_and_agrees`** /
+8. **`response_every_prefix_is_partial_and_agrees`** (incremental vs whole).
+   For a valid drawn message, *every* strict prefix (all split offsets 0..len)
+   must parse as `Ok(Partial)` — never `Err`, never an early `Complete` — and
+   any request-line/status-line fields already populated at a Partial must
+   equal the whole-buffer parse's fields. The whole buffer parses
+   `Complete(len)`. This is the README's streaming contract and a strong
+   in-crate differential with no external oracle.
+
+9. **`complete_result_unchanged_by_trailing_bytes`** (completeness
+   monotonicity). If a buffer parses as `Complete(n)`, appending arbitrary
+   drawn junk yields the identical `Complete(n)` and identical parsed fields,
+   for both requests and responses — the parser never reads past the head.
+
+10. **`header_capacity_honored`**. A message with k drawn headers parses
+    `Complete` with exactly k headers when given >= k slots, and returns
+    `Err(TooManyHeaders)` when given fewer (slot count drawn 0..=k+3, so both
+    sides of the boundary are hit, including 0 slots with 0 headers).
+
+11. **`nul_byte_is_always_rejected`** (generate-valid-then-corrupt
+    validation). Replacing any single drawn position of a valid request or
+    response with NUL makes the parse return `Err` — NUL is forbidden in
+    every position of an HTTP/1.x message head.
+
+12. **`forbidden_header_byte_is_rejected`** (token-set boundary). Inserting
+    any byte outside the allowed set for a header name (excluding the
+    structural `:`/CR/LF, which merely re-delimit the message) or a header
+    value (excluding CR/LF) at any drawn offset in a valid request makes the
+    parse fail. The forbidden sets are computed by complementing the crate's
+    own byte maps, so every boundary byte (e.g. `(`, `@`, DEL, 0x0B/0x0C,
+    space-in-name) is sampled.
+
+13. **`only_empty_lines_is_partial`**. Input consisting only of empty lines
+    (CRLF or bare LF, 0..=64 of them, optionally ending in a dangling CR)
+    never completes: both parsers report `Partial` (the documented
+    `skip_empty_lines` behavior). Covers the empty-input case (n = 0).
+
+14. **`chunk_size_roundtrip`**. Any `u64` rendered as hex — either case,
+    with drawn leading zeros up to the parser's 16-digit limit — optionally
+    followed by LWS (SP/HTAB) and a chunk extension (`;` + arbitrary
+    non-CR octets), terminated by CRLF and followed by arbitrary trailing
+    bytes, parses to exactly `Complete((pos_just_past_CRLF, size))`.
+    Exercises the digit-count overflow guard boundary (16 digits of `f` =
+    `u64::MAX`) and the LWS/extension state machine.
+
+15. **`chunk_size_never_panics_on_arbitrary_bytes`**. Raw bytes plus a
+    chunk-ish branch (hex/CR/LF/`;`/LWS alphabet, optionally
+    CRLF-terminated so the Complete branch is actually reachable);
+    `Complete((pos, _))` implies `pos <= len`.
+
+### Generator notes
+
+- All token generators are derived from the crate's own byte maps
+  (`TOKEN_MAP`, `URI_MAP`, `HEADER_VALUE_MAP`, the RFC 7230 reason set), so
+  generation sits exactly at the allowed/forbidden boundary, and the
+  rejection tests sample the *complement* of those maps.
+- Paths mix visible ASCII with arbitrary non-ASCII scalars (`one_of!` over
+  codepoint ranges) since `parse_uri` additionally UTF-8-validates.
+- Messages draw CRLF vs bare-LF line endings, 0..=2 leading empty lines, and
+  0- to 8-header blocks; corruption is a drawn schedule of
+  replace/insert/delete/truncate ops.
+
+## Test results
+
+- `cargo test`: all 383 tests pass (114 lib tests including the 15 hegel
+  properties, 263 `tests/uri.rs` integration tests, 6 doctests).
+- **Liveness / guarded-branch checks**: the `Complete`-branch invariants are
+  behind a guard, so per the skill each guard was verified to fire by
+  inverting the assertion inside it: the request-side and response-side
+  checkers and the `parse_headers` checker all failed immediately with shrunk
+  counterexamples (e.g. the minimal complete response). The
+  `chunk_size_never_panics` Complete guard did *not* fire at the default
+  count with raw bytes — the generator was enriched with a chunk-ish branch
+  until the inverted assertion failed reliably. A plain-assertion liveness
+  check (off-by-one in `chunk_size_roundtrip`'s expected position) shrank to
+  the minimal `"0\r\n"` counterexample. All mutations reverted; `.hegel/`
+  removed afterwards.
+- **Exploratory 10x run**: all 15 properties were run once with
+  `test_cases = 1000`, with a temporary per-case counter in the shaped test
+  confirming 1000 cases actually executed. All passed. Committed tests use
+  the default count.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+None found. Consistent with expectations for a mature, heavily-fuzzed parser:
+the corrupted-message and prefix differentials, byte-set-boundary injections,
+and the 10x run all came back clean. Two leniencies were observed while
+reading the source and deliberately *not* reported as bugs, since both are
+documented in code comments as intentional: chunk extensions accept arbitrary
+octets including a bare LF (comment in `parse_chunk_size` says everything up
+to CR is ignored), and `URI_MAP` accepts all of `!`..=0x7E plus 0x80..=0xFF
+(the source carries a `TODO: Make a stricter checking for URI string?`).
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+None abandoned; several were designed around documented non-guarantees from
+the start rather than asserted naively:
+
+- Header-value round-trip compares against the OWS-trimmed value (the parser
+  documents trimming leading/trailing SP/HTAB).
+- Response reason round-trip expects `""` for absent or obs-text reasons
+  (documented on `Response::reason`), and the subslice check exempts empty
+  slices because that `""` is a static string, not a view into the input.
+- The forbidden-byte injection excludes `:`, CR and LF, which are structural:
+  they re-delimit the message into a *different* (possibly valid) one rather
+  than making the header invalid.
+- The chunk-extension generator excludes only CR, because the parser
+  documents accepting any other octet there.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The Rust reference's Setup section predicted the MSRV `cargo add` failure
+  and `--ignore-rust-version` fixed it exactly as described.
+- The Run-and-Reflect guidance on *vacuous guarded branches* earned its keep:
+  the chunk-size no-panic property's `Complete` guard silently never fired at
+  the default case count, and only the invert-the-inner-assertion check
+  exposed that; the documented remedy (construct guard-satisfying inputs
+  directly) resolved it.
+- The crate's `#![cfg_attr(test, deny(warnings))]` caused no friction with
+  `#[hegel::test]`-generated code — no `#[allow]`s were needed.
+- The "no global case-count override" gotcha was again the only mechanical
+  annoyance for the 10x run (temporary `sed` over the attributes, plus a
+  temporary case counter to prove the higher count ran, per the skill).
+- Shrinking was fast and minimal throughout (e.g. the mutated chunk
+  round-trip shrank to `"0\r\n"`, the shaped-message mutation to the
+  smallest complete message).
